---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import BuildUpLink from "../../components/UnderTheHood/BuildUpLink.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import BusyToggle from "../../components/Blog/BusyToggle.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
import Buttons from "../../component-examples/spinner/Buttons.astro"
---

<TechniquePost slug="spinner-aria-busy">
  <section id="the-problem">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      A loading state is usually two things to keep in sync: a spinner element
      or class for sighted users, and <code>aria-busy</code> for assistive technology.
      Forget one and the two disagree.
    </p>
    <p>If the attribute is already there, it can be the only switch.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="the-trick">
    <h2>The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>[aria-busy="true"]</code> is the selector, no class and no extra element
      </li>
      <li>
        A <code>::before</code> circle with one transparent border side is the ring,
        {" "}<code>rotate(1turn)</code> in a <code>linear</code>, <code
          >infinite</code
        > loop spins it
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>1em</code> sizes it from the font and <code>currentColor</code> colors
        it from the text. The ring is at least the thick border width and grows with
        the font
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>:not(…)</code> skips form fields, <code>&lt;progress&gt;</code>,
        the root and anything but a button or link with <code
          >aria-describedby</code
        >, such as a section pointing to its progress bar
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>:not(:empty)</code> adds a gap only next to text, and skips the library's
        button, which has its own gap
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`[aria-busy="true"]:not(input, select, textarea, html, progress, [aria-describedby]:not(button, a)) {
  &::before {
    animation: spin 0.7s linear infinite;
    block-size: 1em;
    border-block-color: transparent currentColor;
    border-inline-color: currentColor;
    border-radius: 50%;
    border-style: solid;
    border-width: max(var(--ui-border-width-thick), 0.1875em);
    content: "";
    display: inline-block;
    inline-size: 1em;
    opacity: 0.5;
    vertical-align: -0.14em;
  }

  &:not(button.button):not(:empty)::before {
    margin-inline-end: 0.5em;
  }
}

@keyframes spin {
  to {
    transform: rotate(1turn);
  }
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="try-it">
    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Flip the switch. It sets <code>aria-busy</code> on a button, a paragraph and
      a text field, and nothing else changes.
    </p>
    <Example showCode={false}>
      <BusyToggle />
    </Example>
    <ul>
      <li>The button and the paragraph get a ring, the field doesn't</li>
      <li>
        <code>:not(input, …)</code> skips it, because a spinning ring inside a field
        would sit on the text
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Buttons with <code>aria-busy=&quot;true&quot;</code>, with and without a
      visible label.
    </p>
    <Example column showCode={false}>
      <Buttons />
    </Example>
    <BuildUpLink />
  </section>

  <section id="fallback">
    <h2>Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>:not()</code> with a selector list is Selectors Level 4. A browser without
        it drops the rule: no spinner, but <code>aria-busy</code> is still announced
      </li>
      <li>
        Under reduced motion it spins at half speed instead of stopping, so it
        still reads as busy
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
